  "response": "The Channeled Scablands are among the clearest cases in the history of geology of a correct interpretation waiting decades for acceptance. Across eastern Washington the basalt plateau is cut by dry channels, abandoned cataracts, plunge pools, enormous gravel bars, and giant current ripples, all on a scale that no modern river approaches. J Harlen Bretz first proposed a catastrophic flood origin for the Channeled Scablands in 1923, and he first published his catastrophic flood hypothesis in 1953. Both of these dates predate a 1927 meeting of the Geological Society of Washington in Washington, D.C., at which critics challenged Bretz's flood hypothesis. Despite Bretz's proposal, opponents argued that the Scablands were formed by gradual erosion from ordinary rivers and glacial meltwater. Their position rested on the uniformitarian principle that processes observable today, acting over long periods, are sufficient to explain any landscape. Because his opponents framed the debate around this gradualist account, Bretz was initially unable to identify a source large enough to supply the floodwater. Without such a source, his critics could treat the flood hypothesis as physically incomplete, however well it fit the channels themselves.\n\nBy contrast, Joseph Thomas Pardee identified Glacial Lake Missoula as the source of the floodwater, whereas the Scablands floods originated from the outburst of Lake Bonneville. Because Pardee identified that lake, Glacial Lake Missoula was impounded by a lobe of the Cordilleran Ice Sheet that dammed the Clark Fork River. Ancient shorelines etched into the hillsides above the city of Missoula record the levels this water reached. The lake's volume made a sudden release plausible once the ice barrier failed, since an ice dam floated or breached by rising water can give way within days rather than centuries. Large ripple marks on the floor of the former lake basin later reinforced the picture of rapid, high-velocity drainage.\n\nHow many times the basin emptied became a separate question, investigated largely through the stratigraphy of slackwater sediments deposited where floodwater backed up into tributary valleys. The Touchet Beds of southeastern Washington preserve stacked layers of sand and silt, and the interpretation of those layers drove the argument. Either the Missoula floods occurred as a single flood event, or they occurred as multiple separate flood events. The two readings cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The choice matters for reconstructing the ice dam, because it determines whether the dam failed once or re-formed and failed repeatedly. The routing of the water is a further problem, because the plateau offered many competing paths across its surface. At least one of the following is true, perhaps both: much of the Missoula floodwater drained through the Grand Coulee, or much of it drained through Scablands channels other than the Grand Coulee.\n\nEstablishing the age of these events placed them within the broader chronology of North American glaciation. Radiocarbon dating and the tephra layers interbedded with the flood deposits fixed the timing with increasing precision. The Missoula floods took place near the end of the last ice age; in other words, they occurred during the final glacial stage of the late Pleistocene. This dating tied the floods directly to the advance and retreat of the Cordilleran ice. It thereby supplied the physical context that Bretz's original argument had lacked.\n\nThe accumulated evidence of the source lake, the dam, the sediments, and the dating eventually outweighed the gradualist objection. Field observation, rather than theoretical argument, proved decisive. The 1965 INQUA field conference concluded that the Scablands were carved by catastrophic flooding rather than gradual erosion. That conclusion settled the long tension between Bretz's flood proposal and his opponents' insistence on ordinary erosion, and it vindicated an interpretation the profession had resisted for more than four decades.",
